[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16, mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the dynamic routing plane (OSPF/FRR) between the campus L3 switch (mswitch0) and the aggregation router (router16). Mechanism: PCs in VLANs 10/20/30 send off-subnet traffic to their SVI gateway on mswitch0 (172.16.x.254), which can ARP/respond locally. But mswitch0 has only connected routes and no default/upstream routes, so it immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” for anything beyond, making the campus appear isolated beyond router16. Localization: router16 shows only connected routes and no FRR configuration; its FRR service is failed. Evidence: router16 frr.service = failed; FRR running-config is empty (no OSPF), and “show ip route” contains only C 10.2.2.0/24 (bond0) and C 172.30.1.0/24 (eth2). On mswitch0, the SVI gateways are up and respond; however FRR is inactive (dead) despite an OSPF config, and its routing table contains only connected networks (10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10/20/30). Probes: pc5 -> 192.168.10.1 fails with “From 172.16.10.254 Destination Net Unreachable”; mswitch0 cannot reach upstream (ping 172.30.1.2 returns “Network is unreachable”), but can reach router16’s 10.2.2.1.

Root cause: FRR on router16 was left failed/disabled after maintenance, removing OSPF adjacency and any upstream/default routes from mswitch0 (and FRR is also stopped on mswitch0).

Corrective fix: Restore dynamic routing. Start/enable FRR on router16 and mswitch0, and reinstate OSPF for 10.2.2.0/24 (mswitch0–router16) and 172.30.1.0/24 (router16–router7). Alternatively, as a temporary mitigation, add a static default on mswitch0 via 10.2.2.1. Verify OSPF neighbors form and that mswitch0 learns backbone routes before closing. === END ===